          "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/NotThatKindOfDrLiz/events{/privacy}",
          "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/NotThatKindOfDrLiz/received_events",
          "type": "User",
          "user_view_type": "public",
          "site_admin": false
        },
        "body": "This rollback should not merge as-is.\n\nThe core issue is that it removes the current origin boundary for every browser caller, including the token-protected /api/v1/* routes, by replacing the allowlist logic with Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * everywhere.\n\nA few specifics:\n\n- Bearer auth and CORS are not interchangeable controls. Auth still protects the endpoints, but the existing CORS policy is intentionally limiting which browser origins can use browser-held tokens against this API. Replacing that with * is not security-neutral.\n- This worker is described in the repo as a machine-to-machine API first, not a general-purpose browser API. Non-browser clients do not need CORS at all, so reopening CORS globally only broadens browser access.\n- The current allowlist is not just dead complexity. It is preserving a narrower browser exposure model for authenticated routes while still allowing known app and preview origins.\n\nRequested change:\n\n- Keep wildcard CORS only for genuinely public endpoints such as /health and /check-result/:sha256.\n- Preserve the existing allowlist behavior for authenticated /api/v1/* routes.\n- Update tests to reflect that split behavior.\n\nIf there is a concrete new browser origin that legitimately needs authenticated access, add it explicitly or make the allowlist configurable. But please do not reopen authenticated routes to arbitrary web origins with a blanket * response.\n\nOnce the PR is narrowed to public-route wildcard CORS and protected-route allowlisted CORS, this will be in good shape.",
        "commit_id": "1a13e379c897f3bbb2440e3e5020514c49ced349",
